<InsightArticle title="Running local AI on a laptop means checking the file, memory and connection" description="Compare Ollama and LM Studio for a first local-model exercise, with practical checks for memory, offline behaviour and a useful result." date="2026-11-14" type="Explainer" readTime="7 min" snapshot="2026-11-14" standfirst="Compare Ollama and LM Studio for a first local-model exercise, with practical checks for memory, offline behaviour and a useful result.">
<p>Nine of the 268 language models remaining after filtering our frozen AIRH catalogue have no recorded pricing source. That missing field does not tell you whether a model is free, downloadable or suitable for your laptop.</p>
<p>Local AI means running the model on your own machine for the task in question. You still need software, a compatible model file and enough working memory. A hosted model with a generous context limit does not become a laptop model because its name appears in the same directory. The download, licence and runtime need checking separately.</p>
<p>For a first exercise, use a short piece of non-sensitive text and ask for a summary you can compare with the original. That gives you something useful to inspect without beginning with the hardest document on your drive. This guide compares two routes from their documentation, read on 14 November 2026. We have not installed them for this article or measured laptop performance.</p>
<h2>Missing price provenance says nothing about a downloadable model</h2>
<div class="overflow-x-auto"><table><caption>Frozen AIRH catalogue, 14 November 2026. Selections are defined on the transparency page.</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Recorded pricing source</th><th scope="col">Records</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">OpenRouter models API</th><td>201</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Lab's own pricing page</th><td>58</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">none recorded</th><td>9</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The table groups the filtered catalogue by its recorded pricing source. These labels describe our records. They are not a licence classification, a count of locally runnable models or an inventory of free software. We have deliberately avoided deriving a local-model shortlist from those fields, because they do not answer the relevant question.</p>
<p>A candidate for local use needs a model file supported by the chosen application and a licence that permits your intended use. The model publisher's information should identify both. A download page maintained by somebody else may provide a convenient conversion, but it also introduces another party whose files and description you need to understand. The familiar model name alone is insufficient.</p>
<p>The laptop's available memory matters as well as its advertised total. Your operating system and other applications already use some of it. A model file's download size does not describe the whole running process. The context held during a conversation and the software doing the calculation also need memory. That is why a universal claim that a particular laptop size runs a particular model would be misleading here.</p>
<h2>LM Studio offers a visible route into a local conversation</h2>
<p><a href="https://lmstudio.ai/docs/app/offline">LM Studio's offline documentation</a>, read on 14 November 2026, says downloaded models, document chat and a local server can work without an internet connection. Discovering and downloading models require connectivity. That distinction is useful for planning a first session: get the required files before trying the offline part.</p>
<p>For someone who prefers a graphical application, the practical advantage is being able to see the selected model and the conversation together. Before asking a question, check which file is loaded. Similar names may describe different sizes or conversions. Save that exact identifier in your notes so that a later attempt does not silently compare a different file.</p>
<p>Begin with a short request. Ask for a summary of the supplied text and then compare each claim with the original. The first goal is a working local conversation whose output you can inspect. A long wait or an unhelpful answer is useful evidence about that setup, even when the model loaded successfully.</p>
<p>The documentation's statement about local operation applies to the described functions. Extra integrations or remote services need their own check. A local application can still be used as one part of a wider connected workflow. For a simple first exercise, leaving those additions out makes it easier to understand where the text goes.</p>
<h2>Ollama exposes where the model is running</h2>
<p><a href="https://docs.ollama.com/faq">Ollama's local FAQ</a>, read on 14 November 2026, describes the command ollama ps for inspecting loaded models. Its Processor column distinguishes a model loaded on the graphics processor, on the main processor or across both. That is a useful diagnostic when a download succeeds but the conversation is slower than expected.</p>
<p>The graphics processor is often called the GPU. Its dedicated memory, where present, is separate from ordinary system memory. Some machines use a shared arrangement instead, so two laptops with the same headline memory capacity need not behave alike. The application and hardware documentation should settle what your machine supports before you plan around acceleration.</p>
<p><a href="https://docs.ollama.com/gpu">Ollama's hardware support page</a>, read on 14 November 2026, lists supported graphics hardware and driver requirements. Check the current entry for the actual device rather than assuming the laptop's brand is enough. This guide does not recommend a driver change or certify any particular machine as compatible.</p>
<p>Once the model is loaded, the same short summary exercise can reveal whether the setup is usable. Keep the original text and prompt unchanged when comparing applications. Record the file used and what the runtime reports. If the two applications load different conversions, describe the result as a comparison of those setups, rather than attributing the difference solely to the interface.</p>
<h2>A large context setting can change the memory problem</h2>
<p>Context is the text the model can hold in view for a request, including instructions and relevant conversation. A model publisher may advertise a large maximum, while the application uses a smaller setting. Ollama's FAQ documents ways of changing the context setting. The existence of a setting does not establish that your laptop can use the largest value comfortably.</p>
<p>For the first exercise, a short document keeps this variable manageable. If the task later requires more text, increase the amount deliberately and observe whether the application still behaves acceptably. A successful short summary establishes a narrow result. It does not prove that a folder of long reports will fit or that the model will retrieve every important detail.</p>
<p>Document chat may also use retrieval: the application finds selected passages and supplies them to the model. That is different from placing the whole document in view at once. A question about an omitted passage can fail for reasons beyond the model's writing ability. When a result is wrong, check what material the application actually supplied where that information is available.</p>
<p>Quantisation is another term you will meet on download pages. It refers to representing model values with reduced numerical precision, often to reduce storage and memory requirements. Different conversions can trade resource use against output behaviour. Rather than treating a smaller download as automatically equivalent, keep the conversion name with the result and assess it on the task you intend to repeat.</p>
<h2>Offline use needs an offline attempt</h2>
<p>After downloading the software and model, try the same cleared task with connectivity disabled if offline operation is a requirement. A successful request then supports a statement about that particular setup and task. It is stronger evidence than a local-looking window while the computer remains connected, though it is not a complete security audit.</p>
<p>The result also needs to remain useful. Read the summary against the original and mark omissions or invented details. If you spend longer repairing it than writing your own note, the setup has not yet earned a place in that workflow. A different model or narrower task may help, but that is a new attempt to evaluate rather than a guaranteed remedy.</p>
<p>For laptop use, observe ordinary practicalities during the trial: responsiveness while other work is open, heat, fan noise and whether you need mains power. These observations are specific to the machine and workload. We provide no battery-life or speed estimate here because none was measured for this guide.</p>
<p>Keep the working model identifier, application version, prompt and a cleared example together. That small record makes a later update easier to assess. You can return to the same task and see what changed instead of relying on memory of how impressive the first answer felt. Local control becomes useful when you can reproduce a result you understand.</p>
